# KNOWN GAP — MEMORY-UNSAFE, BOTH BACKENDS: `[]` is not gated on the `Index`
# trait, so indexing a type that implements NO indexing trait is silently
# accepted and lowered as an UNCHECKED RAW OFFSET READ.
#
# `Pair` equips nothing. `gg check` reports "OK: no semantic errors"; both
# backends build and run.
#
# MEASURED AT HEAD (2026-08-03), `print(f"{p[0]} {p[1]} {p[5]}")`:
#   C    : 11 22 281474539212408
#   LLVM : 11 22 281473722332240
#
# So p[0]/p[1] read fields a/b by offset, and p[5] reads OUT OF BOUNDS past
# the struct (the value is ASLR-dependent; the stable fact is that it is an
# unchecked read of memory the program does not own).
#
# ROOT — and this is a CLASS, not an instance. `Index[K,V]` (`V get(self, K)`)
# and `IndexMut[K,V]` exist as builtin traits (`src/semantic/traits.rs:754`,
# `:765`), but the `[]` operator never consults them as a gate. Consequences
# already filed separately are the SAME defect in other costumes:
#   - `Set[int]` `s[0]` prints a raw address (known_gaps/set_index_returns_garbage.gg,
#     and the D38 3-lane entry) — Set implements no indexing trait either.
# Fixing at the operator (require an `Index` impl, per Layering discipline:
# the trait IS the typed metadata, read it at one source of truth) closes
# every costume at once. Adding a bespoke Set-index rejection would fix the
# instance and leave this one live — Core #4.
#
# INTENDED: a check-time rejection naming the missing `Index` impl. The
# expected output below is the diagnostic; the harness asserts a rejection,
# so the exact wording is free to improve.
#
# ⚠ Probe discipline (AGENTS.md Core #15(d)): read the out-of-bounds value off
# `gg build` + run, never off `gg run` alone, and check BOTH backends — this
# reproduces identically on C and LLVM, which by Core #8 makes it >= 2 bugs,
# not "benign because both agree".
struct Pair:
    int a
    int b

int main():
    Pair p = Pair(11, 22)
    print(f"{p[0]} {p[1]} {p[5]}")
    return 0
